{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nVanya has a scales for weighing loads and weights of masses w0,\u2009w1,\u2009w2,\u2009...,\u2009w100 grams where w is some integer not less than 2 (exactly one weight of each nominal value). Vanya wonders whether he can weight an item with mass m using the given weights, if the weights can be put on both pans of the scales. Formally speaking, your task is to determine whether it is possible to place an item of mass m and some weights on the left pan of the scales, and some weights on the right pan of the scales so that the pans of the scales were in balance.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new Main().process();\n    }\n\n    private void process() {\n        try (Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n             PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(System.out)) {\n            long w = sc.nextLong();\n            long m = sc.nextLong();\n\n            if (m == 1) {\n                pw.println(\"YES\");\n                return;\n            }\n\n            Set<Long> reachable = new HashSet<>();\n            reachable.add(1L);\n            long wPow = w;\n\n            for (int step = 1; step <= 60; step++) {\n                Set<Long> temp = new HashSet<>(reachable);\n                for (Long val : reachable) {\n                    temp.add(val + wPow);\n                    temp.add(Math.abs(val - wPow));\n                }\n                reachable = temp;\n                if (reachable.contains(m)) {\n                    pw.println(\"YES\");\n                    return;\n                }\n                if (wPow > m * w) break;\n                wPow *= w;\n            }\n\n            pw.println(\"NO\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new Main().process();\n    }\n\n    private void process() {\n        try (Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n             PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(System.out)) {\n            long w = sc.nextLong();\n            long m = sc.nextLong();\n\n            // We need to check if we can balance mass m using weights w^0, w^1, ..., w^100\n            // The key insight: each weight can be on left pan (with the item), right pan, or not used\n            // This corresponds to coefficients -1, 1, or 0 in the equation: m = sum(c_i * w^i) where c_i \u2208 {-1, 0, 1}\n            \n            // Convert m to base w representation and check if it can be balanced\n            while (m > 0) {\n                long remainder = m % w;\n                if (remainder == 0) {\n                    // No weight needed at this position\n                    m /= w;\n                } else if (remainder == 1) {\n                    // Place weight w^i on right pan (opposite to item)\